ROAST.PET WEIRD BUILDER PROJECTS =E2=80=94 2026-09-19 03:21 Asia/Phnom_Penh

SEARCH COVERAGE
Screened 20+ plausible candidates across GitHub plus adjacent
author/dependency trails, Hugging Face-oriented searches, pet
landmarks, browser lip sync, browser matting, WebAR, Etsy
personalization, POD/mockups and video assembly. Aggressively
deduplicated against earlier Roast.pet scans. Most new results were
weaker duplicates of Catsync, HeadTTS, MotionPNGTuber, MindAR,
Alphaveil/Cutout Studio, DogFLW/CAFM and existing mockup/video
tooling. Three new mechanisms are worth retaining.

1) HeadAudio =E2=80=94 met4citizen / Mika Suominen
URL: https://github.com/met4citizen/HeadAudio
Activity: current crawl Sep 2026; repo exposes 1 main commit plus
training/tests; author actively maintains adjacent HeadTTS/TalkingHead
ecosystem.
License: MIT.
Solves: fallback lip sync for ANY audio source without transcript,
phoneme timestamps, Rhubarb or server inference. Browser AudioWorklet
maps raw audio to 15 Oculus visemes in real time.
Install/API/demo: npm/browser modules; load headaudio.mjs +
headworklet.mjs + supplied model-en-mixed.bin. Connect an AudioNode
and consume onvalue/onviseme callbacks.
Dependencies/hardware: no external runtime dependencies; fully
browser-side; tested Chrome/Edge/Firefox/Safari desktop and
iPad/iPhone. Supplied classifier model is only ~14 kB. MFCC +
Gaussian/Mahalanobis classifier; no GPU.
Evidence: README documents live test app, OpenAI realtime demo,
benchmark timings and complete training pipeline. Typical processor
time ~0.035 ms per 128-sample block in author benchmark, ~50 ms
end-to-end prediction latency.
Work replaced: almost the entire external-audio fallback pipeline:
upload audio -> alignment/STT -> phonemes -> visemes. Primary
generated Kokoro audio should still use HeadTTS's exact model-derived
timings; HeadAudio is the tiny universal fallback for
customer-supplied/changed audio.
Integration: LOW.
Maintenance risk: LOW-MED; tiny implementation, but audio-driven
classification is explicitly less accurate than text/timestamp-driven
lip sync.
Exact experiment: render 20 roast lines twice: HeadTTS exact visemes
vs HeadAudio listening to the resulting WAV. Collapse both 15-viseme
streams into the same 6 pet-mouth states and blind-score sync. Then
test one arbitrary MP3 to verify zero-transcript fallback.
Decision: REUSE NOW AS FALLBACK.

Surprising adjacency: HeadAudio was trained using HeadTTS-generated
WAV + exact phoneme/viseme timestamps. Four Kokoro voices generated
roughly 40 minutes of labeled audio; Gaussian prototypes compile into
a ~14 kB model. This suggests we could cheaply train a PET-SPECIFIC
REDUCED VISEME CLASSIFIER if 15 mouth classes prove unnecessary:
synthesize labeled Kokoro speech, map to 5=E2=80=936 exaggerated pet mouths=
,
compile tiny prototypes. Do not do this until the supplied model is
benchmarked.

2) Charley3d/lip-sync =E2=80=94 Blender Lip Sync 2D
URL: https://github.com/Charley3d/lip-sync
Creator: Charley3d.
Activity: current crawl Sep 2026; 61 stars / 11 forks.
License: GPL-3.0-or-later.
Solves: projection of a viseme spritesheet onto an explicitly selected
mouth area, plus smooth interpolation between mouth states. This is a
mature independent implementation of the exact 'generate only tiny
mouth patches, not whole pet faces' mechanism Roast.pet converged on.
Install/API/demo: install as Blender 4.4+ add-on; import audio; select
mouth area in Edit Mode; add spritesheet; Analyze Audio. Ships demo
videos.
Dependencies/hardware: Blender; offline Vosk + Phonemizer + eSpeak;
~40 MB language model. CPU suitable.
Evidence: README includes demos, Blender extension installation,
custom spritesheet support, ~25=E2=80=9330 languages, shape-key and
projected-sprite modes.
Work replaced: not production runtime; it can replace our manual
prototyping/calibration environment for answering whether projected
mouth patches look convincing on pets and whether smooth interpolation
beats hard sprite switching.
Integration: LOW for experiment, HIGH/undesirable as production
dependency because Blender + GPL.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM.
Exact experiment: import one canonical pet plane/model, define mouth
region, use our generated 6/9 mouth spritesheet, bake lip sync,
compare hard switch vs interpolated projection and MotionPNGTuber's
quadrilateral warp. If Blender interpolation looks materially better,
copy the mathematical idea rather than source.
Decision: COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK, not embed.

3) LiveToon =E2=80=94 oaboelazm
URL: https://github.com/oaboelazm/LiveToon
Creator: oaboelazm.
Activity: current crawl Sep 2026.
License: repository says see LICENSE; verify exact terms before source reus=
e.
Solves: a compact, explicit AnimationState abstraction for blink,
mouth ratio, smile, eyebrow, yaw/pitch/roll with One Euro filtering
and alpha-blended sprite layers. Human MediaPipe tracking is
irrelevant to Roast.pet production, but the state
smoothing/threshold/calibration machinery is directly reusable for
deterministic pet reactions.
Install/API/demo: Python/Gradio; one-click Colab/Kaggle path; upload
PNG and webcam/video.
Dependencies/hardware: OpenCV, MediaPipe, NumPy, SciPy, Pillow,
Gradio; CPU, >=3D20 FPS claimed.
Evidence: runnable Gradio/Kaggle/Colab workflow; documented
architecture and debug overlay; hot-swap avatars and personalized
threshold calibration.
Work replaced: animation-state smoothing and debug instrumentation.
Specifically, One Euro filtering and a single AnimationState object
can stop blink/head-bob/side-eye/recoil transforms from looking
jittery when driven by noisy landmarks or audio energy.
Integration: LOW if copying the mechanism, MED if adopting code.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM.
Exact experiment: take our deterministic pet renderer and route head
bob, blink and recoil values through the same One Euro smoothing
parameters (min cutoff 1.5, beta 0.05 as documented). A/B against raw
keyframes on 10 roasts. Keep only if motion feels less mechanical
without blurring punchline reactions.
Decision: COPY MECHANISM.

IMPORTANT NEGATIVE / LICENSING RESULT
DogFLW is scientifically excellent (4,335 dog-face images, 46
landmarks chosen around dog facial musculature/DogFACS), but its
dataset is CC BY-NC 4.0. Do NOT train a commercial Roast.pet model
directly on DogFLW without separate permission. It remains useful as a
research/evaluation reference. This strengthens the earlier
recommendation to prefer commercially safe pet-alignment weights or
simple bbox/eye/nose models rather than casually training on academic
pet datasets.
URL: https://github.com/martvelge/DogFLW

ETSY UPDATE WORTH LOCKING INTO PRODUCT DESIGN
Etsy's 2026 personalization migration is now GA architecture: up to
five typed personalization questions, including one file-upload
question that can accept up to 10 files. This means Roast.pet should
not build its own pre-purchase image uploader merely to collect pet
photos if Etsy-native personalization fits the flow. The API migration
moved personalization into dedicated endpoints and requires
integrations to support the new response format.
Reference: https://github.com/etsy/open-api/discussions/1581

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES
1. External-audio phoneme/viseme fallback: HeadAudio is a ~14 kB
browser classifier and works without transcripts.
2. A custom mouth-projection research rig: Blender Lip Sync 2D already
lets us test spritesheet projection/interpolation immediately.
3. Animation smoothing primitives: copy One Euro/state-calibration
mechanisms from LiveToon rather than tuning jitter filters ad hoc.
4. Etsy customer pet-photo collection UI: use native typed/file-upload
personalization wherever the marketplace flow permits.
5. Another academic dog-landmark model trained from scratch: licensing
is messy and existing detectors/alignment primitives are sufficient
for V1.

MOST SURPRISING HOBBY PROJECT
HeadAudio. A 48-star browser project turns arbitrary speech into 15
visemes using a roughly 14 kB Gaussian-prototype model, with no neural
runtime and no server. More importantly, its training recipe
bootstraps labeled data from HeadTTS itself. That is exactly the kind
of tiny primitive that can collapse an annoying fallback path.

FASTEST INTEGRATION WIN
HeadAudio: connect a generated Kokoro WAV to the test app, map 15
Oculus visemes to our 6 pet-mouth states, and compare against HeadTTS
exact timing. This is an hours-not-days experiment.

ONE REPO/AUTHOR WORTH FOLLOWING
Mika Suominen / met4citizen. Following HeadTTS into HeadAudio
uncovered a coherent set of tiny browser avatar primitives:
timestamped Kokoro, text-driven visemes, audio-only viseme fallback,
and TalkingHead. The architecture is unusually aligned with
Roast.pet's goal of deterministic cheap animation.

ARCHITECTURE DELTA
Primary path remains: roast text -> timestamped Kokoro/HeadTTS ->
exact visemes + audio -> tiny mouth patches + deterministic reactions
-> renderer.
New fallback path: arbitrary audio -> HeadAudio (~14 kB model) ->
visemes -> same renderer.
This means both generated and customer-supplied audio can feed the
identical animation interface without Whisper/Rhubarb being mandatory.
